# Should the Domination That Destroys Tamil Continue Any Longer?
Tamil title: தமிழ் அழிக்கும் ஆதிக்கம் இனியும் நீடிப்பதா?

Summary of the speech delivered by Arignar Anna at Muttathur on 29 June 1960:

Comrades!

“Once village people acquire awareness and rise up, no government, however great the military force at its command, can suppress that awakening!

“What surprises all of us is that the rulers remain so unconcerned despite knowing this.

“No section of the population is satisfied with this government. Workers have been subjected to hardship because prices keep rising.

“It is precisely at this time that the northern government is imposing Hindi with mounting haste!

“There are a great many people in this country who do not even know Tamil, their mother tongue.

“Hindi is being imposed rapidly upon our country on the premise that only those who know Hindi can find employment in the North.

“If Hindi is imposed upon our homeland in that way, people whose mother tongue is Hindi will become its rulers, merchants, and officials.

“Just as a person who has lost his eyes cannot see the beauty of a flower or even the wife whose marriage pendant he tied, losing our mother tongue will leave us in a comparable condition.

“Today the northern government has grown bold enough to make things reach the point where employment requires Hindi, promotion requires Hindi, trade requires Hindi, and even a place in an election requires Hindi. In this way, they are imposing Hindi upon us step by step in every sphere.

“A traveller sat down on the raised veranda of a house. When the householder came outside and asked what he wanted, the man replied, ‘I am very tired; please give me a little water to drink.’ The householder brought him water. Later, while the householder was eating inside, the traveller came and sat beside him, and they fed him too. After eating, he said, ‘I am tired and need to lie down for a while,’ and the householder provided a bed. The householder then began to think, ‘At the next stage, this fellow may even ask me to send my wife to fan him.’ That is the condition in which we find ourselves today.

“The northerners came to rule this country, and we said, ‘Very well.’ They demanded taxes, and we paid without refusing. But now they are imposing Hindi!

“We must think about Hindi coming from the North into our country just as that householder thought, ‘It appears that the man who sat upon my veranda will now ask even my wife to come and fan him.’

“Those without honour, pride, or feeling may be willing to fan him. No one who possesses honour, pride, and feeling will do so.

“It is because we possess Tamil literature, the Tamil language, and Tamil civilisation that we are called Tamils. Why are we not called Malayalis? Why are we not called Andhras? Why are we not called Kannadigas?

“What does the term ‘a married woman’ signify? The marriage pendant around her neck and the mark upon her forehead identify her as married. If someone saw her and said, ‘Erase the mark on your forehead,’ she would ask, ‘Wretch! While my husband stands firm as a rock, do you tell me to erase the mark upon my forehead?’

“In the same way, they forcibly impose Hindi upon us and tell us, ‘Destroy Tamil.’

“That is why our friend Ramasami said, ‘If Hindi is imposed, I am prepared to withstand even two bullets.’

“A government shorthand writer and a police shorthand writer are here taking down our speeches. If they want promotion, they must study Hindi. Only by studying Hindi can a sub-inspector become an inspector; only by studying Hindi can an inspector become a circle inspector. However capable they may be, however skilled at detecting theft, even if they say, ‘Was it not I who attended every one of Annadurai’s meetings and wrote shorthand without regard for night or day?’ they will receive no promotion. The first question will be, ‘Do you know Hindi?’ Only if they have studied Hindi will they obtain advancement.

“Even Kamaraj will be asked the same question. He may be capable, but if Ramasami Padayachi leaves, they may select Samikannu Padayachi; if Samikannu Padayachi leaves, they may select Gopala Gounder; and if Gopala Gounder leaves, they may select Venugopala Gounder. Yet, when it comes to assuming power, they will first ask, ‘Do you know Hindi?’ It is on behalf of everyone, therefore, that we say, ‘We do not want Hindi.’

“Thirteen years ago in our country, we regarded the white man as an oppressor. Congress members told us that industry did not develop because he was here; famine and starvation existed because he was here; unemployment existed because he was here; and that if we removed him, we could live prosperously without hunger, famine, starvation, or unemployment. Believing them, we became victims of bullets, shed blood, lost property and comfort, and drove the British out of this country.

“As they departed, the British handed independence to the northerners. Today they sit in the place once occupied by the British and do everything the British did.

“If two measures of rice sold for a rupee under British rule, under this government one measure sells for one rupee and three annas! Clothes that sold for four or five annas under their rule now sell for three or four rupees. Prices have risen to that extent under this government!

“In thirteen years, prices have risen to a level no one expected. Mothers! Elders! I ask you: what is the reason for this?

“The tax money we give is the size of a pumpkin. By the time it passes through officials and reaches the government, it has shrunk to the size of a snake gourd. When it goes from there to the North, it takes the size of an okra. When something finally returns to us, we receive only the stalk! What is the cause of all this?

“Congress members told the imperialists in Delhi about the influence we possess here. They immediately began investigating: ‘What can be done to break their unity? What gives them this unity?’ They realised that the Tamil language was the cause of it all. They therefore concluded: ‘If we bring in Hindi, the influence of Tamil will disappear by itself; Tamil feeling will vanish; without Tamil feeling, they will not demand Dravida Nadu; and without Dravida Nadu, whatever we lay down will become law.’ The imposition of Hindi is the consequence of that decision!

“The Cheras, Cholas, and Pandyas ruled Tamil Nadu. Its literature extends from Aathichudi to Akananuru. What has happened to this Tamil language? Why must Hindi be imposed?

“Suppose a son allows the mother who gave birth to him to beg in Tindivanam while he strolls around a three-storey house in Villupuram. Even a passer-by would spit in disgust and say, ‘Poor woman! The mother who bore him begs in Tindivanam while he wanders about a three-storey house here.’ You are all familiar with such ordinary examples.

“They say that studying only Tamil is not enough, and they have fixed 1965 as the deadline. Should we not oppose this?

“When we attend a feast and something we dislike is served upon our leaf, we have the sense to push it aside. Some people, fearing that merely seeing it while they eat will cause disgust, place a handful of rice over it, cover it, and only then continue eating!

“The Tamil language has come down through generation after generation and has been praised throughout the world. If northerners tell us that such a language is unwanted, how can we listen and remain silent?

“We are going to conduct the anti-Hindi struggle to demand Tamil, Tamil Nadu, and Dravida Nadu. All of you must participate in that struggle on a great scale.

“The people of the South quarry and bring the stones. The northerners act as priests who turn those stones into ‘gods,’ install them in temples, and demand offerings from the people who come to worship!

“We possess the fields, while they possess Sindri, where fertiliser for our fields is manufactured!

“Railway carriages are made here, while they manufacture the railway engines that pull them!

“Iron is found here; there are oil-bearing rivers; gold is found here. Yet there are no means here to establish factories using these resources!

“All the great factories are being established only in the North!

“Congress members abuse us precisely because we point out all these things!

“Unable to make a living here, Tamils travel to Ceylon and Burma and labour as coolies on tea estates. If those governments become angry with Tamils, they shoot them down as though hunting animals in a forest. Kamaraj has neither the authority nor the means to question it, neither indignation nor feeling! Yet he says, ‘I have a gun; I will shoot you down.’ To that extent has the tongue God gave our Chief Minister grown long!

“When the government says it has guns with which to shoot, it is like a husband threatening his wife. Like such a husband, Kamaraj looks at us and says, ‘I will have you shot.’

“Japan’s Prime Minister Kishi crowed in the same fashion. Yet that very Kishi, unable to withstand the people’s uprising, submitted his resignation and left. I worry only because such a fate should not befall the pitiable Kamaraj. If he seeks out such a condition himself, there is nothing we can do!

“Therefore, the Dravida Munnetra Kazhagam will never fear such threats and bluster. We have become accustomed to seeing such intimidation and bluster over many long years.

“I therefore appeal to all the people of the country to come forward, participate in the anti-Hindi struggle that the DMK is about to launch, and eradicate Hindi in order to protect Tamil, our mother tongue, and our motherland.”

---NOTES---
The Tamil OCR source is preserved verbatim in the bilingual output. The date was verified directly from Image 1 as 29-6-1960. Clear contextual readings used only in English include “அளுபவர்களாக” as rulers, “அண்டார்கள்” as ruled/came to rule, “அரிதி” as rice, “நபாய்” as rupee, and “எண்ணெய் அற்றுக்கள்” as oil-bearing rivers. “சிந்திரி” refers to Sindri, historically associated with fertiliser production. Political, linguistic, economic, biographical, and historical assertions are translated in the source voice and have not been independently verified.
